/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  batch31_fast_equilibrium_reduced -- the REDUCED twin of
  batch30_fast_equilibrium_full.

  THE REDUCTION.  When A <-> B is fast against B -> C, A and B stay at
  equilibrium with each other, n_B = K n_A, and only their sum moves:

      d(pool)/dt = -k K/(1+K) pool,     pool = n_A + n_B

  So this case runs ONE reaction, pool -> C, at that rate, written as an
  `expression` law in constant/reactions.  Here compA STANDS FOR THE POOL:
  at t = 0 it holds the whole charge, and at any time the share that is B
  is K/(1+K) = 0.8 of it.  compB stays declared (the two cases share one
  component list) and stays at zero.

  WHAT IS KEPT.  Two numbers, k and K.  The fast rate constants kf and kr
  do not appear at all: any pair with the same ratio gives this answer.
  That is the reduction's real gain -- it needs one parameter fewer, and it
  is the parameter that is hardest to measure.  What is lost is the first
  moment, while B forms from A: batch30 measures it.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        reactor;
        type        batchReactor;

        operation
        {
            mode         isothermal;
            T_setpoint   350.0 K;
        }

        reactions  ( pool_to_C );
    }
);
